Title: Admit the smallest attachment first, as upstream 6012 does
Created: 2026-09-16
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-09-16T00:00Z MinhHaDuong created

--- body ---

## Context

Author, 2026-09-16, reconsidering the admission order after the AR6 stall:
starting with the small easy files is also a way to minimise risk, and upstream
does it.

Checked rather than assumed, and both halves hold:

- **Upstream orders smallest-first.** `SPEC.md` C2 already records it: "Zotero's
  own draft PR #6012 orders attachments smallest-first".
- **We do not.** Admission takes the first match, in whatever order
  `refreshQueue()` happened to build the array:
  `state.pending.find(item => !attempted.has(item.id) || attempted.get(item.id)
  !== observed.get(item.id)?.identity)`.

## Why this is more than throughput

Coverage rising faster is the obvious half and the weaker one. The half that
matters is what an admission order does to the cost of a stall.

A document can wedge extraction and **nothing on this side can end it** — the
worker accepts no abort signal, and the sitter's own code says there is no
check "from here to this document's own settlement". Ticket 0793 exists to
establish whether the AR6 plateau is a slow tail or a wedge, and the honest
reading today is that it is unestablished.

Under that constraint, the admission order is the only lever the sitter has
over what a wedge costs. Largest-last means a wedge takes the tail of a pass;
largest-first means it takes the whole pass. On 2026-09-15 the AR6 ate the end
of a sweep. Admitted first, it would have eaten all of it.

**This is risk management that needs nobody upstream to fix anything**, which
is what makes it worth doing before 0793 reports.

## The field already exists

No new plumbing. `bootstrap.js`'s inspect path already computes
`result.sourceBytes = source.size` from an `IOUtils.stat` it already performs,
and `refreshQueue()` already carries it onto every queue entry alongside
`pages`:

```js
.map(([id, info]) => ({ id, title: ..., parentTitle: ...,
  sourceBytes: info.sourceBytes, pages: info.pages }));
```

It is a trusted field rather than a stray one: the duration-estimate fit
already keys on it, guarded by `Number.isFinite(s.sourceBytes) && s.sourceBytes > 0`.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js` — `refreshQueue()`, and the candidate
  search in `pump()` that calls `state.pending.find(...)`. Cite by symbol: the
  line numbers moved three times on 2026-09-15 alone.
- `tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs` — the suite that drives `pump()` against a
  fixture host.
- `SPEC.md` C2 — where the upstream parity claim already lives.

## The two design decisions this ticket owes

1. **Where the order is applied.** Sorting inside `refreshQueue()` is one line,
   but that function runs from `record()` on every observation, so a bulk sync
   would pay an O(n log n) sort per notification over a queue of thousands.
   Selecting the minimum at admission is O(n) once per document instead.
   Measure before choosing, and say which you chose and why.
2. **Where an unknown size sorts.** `sourceBytes` can be absent when the stat
   failed. Deferring the unknown is the reading consistent with this ticket's
   own argument — an unmeasured document is exactly the one whose cost you
   cannot bound — but it is a decision, not a default, and it needs its own
   assertion.

## The objection that must be answered, not skipped

**Strict smallest-first can starve the largest document indefinitely.** On a
library with continuous small-file churn, a new small attachment can always
arrive before the big one is reached, and the document most in need of being
indexed once is the one never admitted. Either show that the queue drains in
practice so the case cannot arise, or carry an ageing rule so a deferred
document eventually wins. Do not ship strict ordering without saying which.

## Test

Red first, in the scheduler suite. A fixture whose queue holds attachments of
known differing `sourceBytes`, admitted in a recorded order, and asserted to be
ascending. Against today's code the order is the array's, so seed the queue so
that insertion order and size order disagree — otherwise the test passes
against the unfixed code and proves nothing.

Second test for the starvation objection, matching whichever answer action 2
above gives.

## Verification

- [ ] The order test fails before the change, with the failure recorded
- [ ] A queue whose insertion order already happens to be ascending is NOT what
      the test uses
- [ ] The unknown-size policy has an assertion on its exact behaviour
- [ ] The starvation objection is answered by a test or by a recorded argument
- [ ] `make check` green; scheduler mutants green with a mutant for the order
- [ ] `SPEC.md` C2's parity claim updated to say we now match, if we do

## Invariants

- The `attempted` / identity re-pick logic survives: the candidate search skips
  what was already tried unless its identity changed, and ordering must not
  quietly re-admit a failed document forever.
- 0796's drain budget is untouched.
- No new periodic cost while idle (0795's invariant, and 0701's).

## Exit criteria

Admission takes the smallest unattempted candidate rather than the first, the
unknown-size and starvation cases each have a recorded answer, and a test that
was red on the old order is green on the new one.
